#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231292 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "b71e74ab-9cb3-5e43-94aa-52c2a926dc5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21874" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny''+2y=f(x), \\qquad y'(0)=0,\\quad y'(1)=-5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and even. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_p$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_p$ satisfies $y_p'(0) = 0$ and $y_p'(1) = 0.$*\n![](images/57/579150783e9ae1e69686ff319572bf5523934bf8eb266c5c19e9bf553aa8b0e1.png)" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y=y_c+y_p$, where $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation $y''+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sin(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\ny'(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)+y_p'(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since $y_p'(0)=0$ and $y_p'(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny'(0)=y_c'(0)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y'(1)=y_c'(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y'(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{2}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{2}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_c(x)=A\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use $y'(1)=-5$ with $B=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ -5 & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ A & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt gray]{\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\sqrt{2})}\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)}\n+\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231296 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "f4a74f28-4b3b-5602-b5e5-53c8590d4a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-21887" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny''+8y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-3,3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and even. What Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution $y_p?$" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $6$-periodic on $[-3,3]$, we have $L=3$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for $y_p$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81336ec7-ff98-523f-be77-ba56b479a038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4)/(\\sqrt{5}\\sin (\\sqrt{5}))\\sin (\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230313} {:answer/id #uuid "945d364d-c7f6-5176-bb73-fe7fc885bf62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (4)/(\\sqrt{5}\\sin (\\sqrt{5}))\\cos (\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230314} {:answer/id #uuid "1fdeebe7-48fc-51e3-b898-60a2bbab6dc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (4)/(\\sqrt{5}\\cos (\\sqrt{5}))\\cos (\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230315} {:answer/id #uuid "64c1260a-558f-5457-a363-445abd15d78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4)/(\\sqrt{5}\\sin (\\sqrt{5}))\\cos (\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230316}] :answer-field/id #uuid "95f8400d-4984-52ed-a6b5-76de8e91ccd4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230312} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0437a74-7cb2-5978-b339-e0b17a79e62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372819} {:answer/id #uuid "0f7c971d-59bf-55e6-9232-39b203a6295d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372820} {:answer/id #uuid "243bd63f-3232-5656-b4db-8941b50e55f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372821} {:answer/id #uuid "099d26ea-ed99-5004-b3f7-8e7881f83716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "243bd63f-3232-5656-b4db-8941b50e55f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372821} :answer-field/id #uuid "126b1668-d791-55ec-9027-d2749d759ab6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f970b89-f8e7-590f-9e3b-8ad7dbd8f9e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-343669" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{5}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{5}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 4 & =-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe92f4fa-7615-54d2-a819-127fb1a673f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372862} {:answer/id #uuid "3b55cc73-4e32-591c-bee5-a9d934861abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372863} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4b6aad-9479-5fcd-b841-a17705d991a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372864} {:answer/id #uuid "b3faa44a-d5ae-5692-901c-7fe9f2979668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f4b6aad-9479-5fcd-b841-a17705d991a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372864} :answer-field/id #uuid "f04ef90f-3003-58fb-a7fe-77d398fbdee2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f67e1f7-be25-5035-9c23-854e909c6a11" :question/math-academy-id "q-344268" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}\\,y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-5,5]$, where $f$ is $10$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $10$-periodic on $[-5,5]$, we have $L=5$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c02dac-dc9a-58f5-ac61-be40e997ed00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391886} {:answer/id #uuid "a756987e-8fb9-5f04-9834-f95885d2ee05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391887} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3e1e17-92c5-52da-b301-a2ebd8bda2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391888} {:answer/id #uuid "50a798b3-b743-56ed-9b8e-f33b96e9fcb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d3e1e17-92c5-52da-b301-a2ebd8bda2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391888} :answer-field/id #uuid "9885c458-8f5d-5130-8d86-c8da5b1b3800" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1231bd2-0ea0-512f-a509-a32953caed3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344280" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}\\,y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-2,2]$, where $f$ is $4$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $4$-periodic on $[-2,2]$, we have $L=2$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6804d99e-c0f4-5c32-ae56-000926b7e16d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372874} {:answer/id #uuid "8e347e67-a037-5fda-affa-f5297b6242db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372875} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7e19e2-75f0-51f6-b02d-6e5432fe5a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372876} {:answer/id #uuid "6327d805-8272-54d9-a5e5-49c33f5eaaed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac7e19e2-75f0-51f6-b02d-6e5432fe5a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372876} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d2507fa-b3f4-5d1e-bea9-60146188893d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "356f9e42-beaf-5ba9-bd6f-c8cc225eace9" :question/math-academy-id "q-344318" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $1$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+4y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=3$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 3 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$"}]}
